Ted Nelson was born in 1937 and grew up mostly with his grandparents in Chicago and New York's Greenwich Village. He studied philosophy at Swarthmore College and sociology at Harvard. During college he began to imagine a computer-based writing system that could store the world's knowledge and let readers follow connections between ideas.

## Naming hypertext

In 1963 he coined the words "hypertext" and "hypermedia," and he published them in 1965. His idea was text that branches and links, so a reader can jump from one document to another instead of reading in a straight line. That is exactly what a link on a web page does today.

## Project Xanadu

- **Early 1960s:** Conceived Project Xanadu, a networked system for linked documents. He started calling it Xanadu in 1966.
- **1974:** Self-published *Computer Lib/Dream Machines*, a book that urged ordinary people to understand and use computers.
- **1981:** Published *Literary Machines*, which laid out the Xanadu vision.
- **2002:** Earned a PhD in media and governance from Keio University in Japan.

Xanadu was never finished, and critics called it the longest-running vaporware project in computing. Nelson argued that the web oversimplified his ideas, for example with links that break and only point one way. Many of the problems he predicted are still being worked on.

